Object‑Oriented Programming in JavaScript revolves around creating reusable, modular, and maintainable code by modeling real‑world entities as objects. Key concepts:

Objects: Collections of properties (data) and methods (behavior).

Classes: ES6 introduced class syntax to define blueprints for objects.

Constructors: Special methods used to initialize object instances.

Inheritance: Mechanism to extend functionality from one class to another (extends keyword).

Encapsulation: Bundling data and methods together; controlling access with private fields (#field) or closures.

Polymorphism: Ability of methods to take different forms (method overriding or overloading behavior).

Abstraction: Hiding implementation details and exposing only necessary functionality.

Prototypes: Before ES6 classes, inheritance was achieved via prototype chains.

this keyword: Refers to the current object context; behavior depends on how a function is called.

Static methods/properties: Belong to the class itself, not instances.

🎯 25 Interview‑Based Q&A on JavaScript OOP
Basics
Q: What is OOP in JavaScript?
A: OOP in JS is a paradigm where code is organized into objects containing properties and methods. It allows modularity, reusability, and abstraction, implemented via prototypes or ES6 classes.

Q: How do you create a class in JavaScript?
A: Using the class keyword:

js
class Person {
  constructor(name) { this.name = name; }
  greet() { return `Hello, ${this.name}`; }
}
Q: What is the difference between a class and an object?
A: A class is a blueprint; an object is an instance created from that blueprint.

Q: Explain the role of the constructor in a class.
A: The constructor initializes object properties when a new instance is created.

Q: How is encapsulation achieved in JavaScript?
A: Using private fields (#field), closures, or restricting property access via getters/setters.

Inheritance & Prototypes
Q: How does inheritance work in JavaScript?
A: With extends keyword in classes or prototype chaining in older JS.

Q: What is the prototype chain?
A: A chain of objects used to resolve property/method lookups. If not found in the object, JS looks up its prototype until reaching Object.prototype.

Q: Difference between classical inheritance and prototypal inheritance?
A: Classical uses classes and hierarchies; prototypal uses objects directly as prototypes.

Q: How do you override a method in a subclass?
A: Define the same method name in the subclass; it replaces the parent’s implementation.

Q: What is super keyword used for?
A: To call parent class constructor or methods inside a subclass.

Advanced Concepts
Q: Explain polymorphism in JavaScript.
A: Achieved via method overriding (same method name, different behavior in subclass).

Q: What is abstraction in JS OOP?
A: Hiding implementation details, exposing only necessary methods. Often achieved via abstract base classes or interfaces (conceptually, since JS doesn’t have native interfaces).

Q: How do you define static methods?
A: Using static keyword inside a class. They belong to the class, not instances.

Q: What is the difference between instance methods and static methods?
A: Instance methods are tied to objects; static methods are tied to the class itself.

Q: How do private fields work in ES6 classes?
A: Declared with # prefix, accessible only inside the class.

Practical Scenarios
Q: How would you implement encapsulation without private fields?
A: Using closures:

js
function Counter() {
  let count = 0;
  this.increment = () => ++count;
  this.getCount = () => count;
}
Q: Why is this keyword tricky in JavaScript?
A: Its value depends on how a function is invoked (object method, standalone, arrow function, event handler).

Q: How do arrow functions affect this?
A: Arrow functions don’t bind their own this; they inherit it from the enclosing scope.

Q: Can JavaScript simulate multiple inheritance?
A: Not directly, but mixins can be used to copy properties/methods into classes.

Q: What are mixins?
A: Functions that add reusable behaviors to classes without formal inheritance.

Interview‑Style Deep Dives
Q: How would you design a Shape class hierarchy?
A: Base Shape class with draw() method; subclasses like Circle, Rectangle override draw().

Q: How do you implement method chaining in JS OOP?
A: Return this from methods:

js
class Builder {
  setName(name) { this.name = name; return this; }
}
Q: What is the difference between Object.create() and class syntax?
A: Object.create() directly sets prototype; class provides syntactic sugar for constructor/prototype pattern.

Q: How do you enforce abstraction in JS?
A: By throwing errors in base class methods that must be overridden:

js
class Animal {
  speak() { throw new Error("Must override"); }
}
Q: Why is OOP important in large‑scale JS applications?
A: It improves maintainability, scalability, and readability by organizing code into modular, reusable components.
